---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **「Korean movies on Netflix」에 답하는 자리.** (`/korean-movies-on-netflix`)
 *
 * ── 왜 (2026-08-29) ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * 사장님 지시 — 「영상만 하지말고, **텍스트 콘텐트를 만들어서 검색엔진 통해서 우리 쪽으로
 * 방문하게 해. 색인 잊지말고.**」
 *
 * 잰 수요 — `korean movies on netflix` 자동완성 1번째 · 제안 10줄.
 * 우리 2,723장 가운데 그 물음에 답하는 «제목»이 한 장도 없었다. 있는 것은 다 드라마 이야기였다.
 * ⭐ 영화 줄은 이미 쥐고 있었다. **안 쓰던 축**이었을 뿐이다.
 *
 * ── 이 지면이 말하는 하나 ─────────────────────────────────────
 * **영화가 시리즈보다 «더 많이» 차트에 오른다(577 대 396). 그런데 한 편이 가는 거리가 짧다 —
 * 가운데값으로 1나라·3주 대 5나라·6주.** 목록은 넓고 얇다.
 *
 * ⛔ 「영화가 시리즈보다 못하다」로 쓰지 않는다. 값어치를 판정하지 않는다.
 * ⛔ 「best korean movies」로 제목을 달지 않는다 — 우리가 고를 자격이 없다.
 * ⛔ 차트 자리를 시청시간으로 부르지 않는다.
 * ⚠ 극장 개봉작과 넷플릭스 자체 영화가 한 표에 섞여 있다. 못 가른다고 지면이 스스로 적는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwMethodLimits from '../../components/KcwMethodLimits.astro';
import data from '../../data/kcw-films.json';

const TITLE = 'Korean movies on Netflix: 577 of them charted, and most reached one country';
const DESC = 'Every Korean film that ever entered a country\'s Netflix weekly top 10 — 577 of them, '
  + 'more than the 396 Korean series. But the median film reached one country for three weeks, '
  + 'while the median series reached five. Widest, longest-running and most chart places, listed.';

const 영 = data.films;
const 시 = data.series;
const 몫 = (a: number, b: number) => Math.round((a / b) * 100);

const 표 = [
  { key: 'widest', h: 'The Korean films that reached the most countries',
    what: 'How many of the countries we hold ever put this film in their weekly top 10.',
    rows: data.widest, unit: 'Countries' },
  { key: 'longest', h: 'The Korean films that stayed on a chart longest',
    what: 'How many distinct weeks the film held a top 10 place somewhere. A film in three countries for the same week counts that week once.',
    rows: data.longest, unit: 'Weeks' },
  { key: 'places', h: 'The Korean films that took the most chart places',
    what: 'One place is one appearance in one country in one week. This is reach and staying power multiplied together.',
    rows: data.mostPlaces, unit: 'Places' },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  spatialCoverage: `${data.marketCount} countries`,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/korean-movies-on-netflix">
  <h1>Korean movies on Netflix</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Between {data.weekFrom} and {data.weekTo}, <b>{영.titles} Korean films</b> entered some
    country&rsquo;s Netflix weekly top 10. That is <b>{영.titles - 시.titles} more than the
    {' '}{시.titles} Korean series</b> that did the same over the same weeks. If you had guessed
    that Korean television dominates the catalogue by count, the count says otherwise.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    The distance each one travels is where they part. The median Korean film reached
    {' '}<b>{영.medianCountries} {영.medianCountries === 1 ? 'country' : 'countries'}</b> and held a
    place for <b>{영.medianWeeks} weeks</b>. The median Korean series reached
    {' '}<b>{시.medianCountries} countries</b> and held for <b>{시.medianWeeks} weeks</b>.
    <b> {영.oneCountryOnly} of the {영.titles} films &mdash; {몫(영.oneCountryOnly, 영.titles)}% &mdash;
    charted in exactly one country and nowhere else.</b>
  </p>

  <p class="warn">
    <b>This counts chart places, not viewers.</b> Netflix publishes no hours and no audience by
    country, only a weekly list of ten per country. So a film that charted in forty small markets
    and one that charted in forty large ones look identical on this page. Nothing here is a
    judgement of quality, and we are not going to tell you which Korean film is best &mdash;
    that is not ours to say.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Wide and thin, next to narrow and deep</h2>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th class="num">Films</th><th class="num">Series</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Titles that ever charted</td><td class="num"><b>{영.titles}</b></td><td class="num"><b>{시.titles}</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Median countries reached</td><td class="num">{영.medianCountries}</td><td class="num">{시.medianCountries}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Median weeks on a chart</td><td class="num">{영.medianWeeks}</td><td class="num">{시.medianWeeks}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Median chart places</td><td class="num">{영.medianPlaces}</td><td class="num">{시.medianPlaces}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Charted in one country only</td><td class="num">{영.oneCountryOnly} <span class="fine">({몫(영.oneCountryOnly, 영.titles)}%)</span></td><td class="num">{시.oneCountryOnly} <span class="fine">({몫(시.oneCountryOnly, 시.titles)}%)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lasted exactly one week</td><td class="num">{영.oneWeekOnly} <span class="fine">({몫(영.oneWeekOnly, 영.titles)}%)</span></td><td class="num">{시.oneWeekOnly} <span class="fine">({몫(시.oneWeekOnly, 시.titles)}%)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reached number one somewhere</td><td class="num">{영.everNumberOne}</td><td class="num">{시.everNumberOne}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Have a page on this site</td><td class="num">{영.published}</td><td class="num">{시.published}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fine">
      <b>Reaching number one is not the rare part.</b> {영.everNumberOne} of the {영.titles} films
      have been number one in some country in some week &mdash; number one in one small market for
      one week is common enough that the column separates almost nothing. Reach and duration do
      the separating on this page.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <b>{영.titles - 영.published} of the {영.titles} films have no page here.</b> We publish a
      title page only when we hold enough rows to fill one, and we would rather list a name with
      no link than publish a page that says nothing. Names below without a link are those.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How many first charted each year</h2>
    <table class="yr">
      <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th class="num">Korean films entering a chart for the first time</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.byYear.map((y: { year: number; titles: number }) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{y.year}</td>
            <td class="num">{y.titles}
              <span class="bar" style={`--w:${Math.round((y.titles / Math.max(...data.byYear.map((z: { titles: number }) => z.titles))) * 100)}%`}></span>
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fine">
      The first and last years are partial &mdash; our weeks start {data.weekFrom} and stop
      {' '}{data.weekTo}, so neither covers a full twelve months. We are not going to call the
      shape of this a trend on that basis.
    </p>
  </section>

  {표.map((t) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{t.h}</h2>
      <p class="sub">{t.what} Top {t.rows.length} shown.</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Film</th><th class="num">{t.unit}</th>
            <th class="num">Countries</th><th class="num">Weeks</th>
            <th class="num">At once</th><th>First charted</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {t.rows.map((r: any) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{r.slug ? <a href={`/title/${r.slug}`}>{r.title}</a> : <span>{r.title}</span>}</td>
              <td class="num"><b>{(t.key === 'widest' ? r.countries : t.key === 'longest' ? r.weeks : r.places).toLocaleString('en-US')}</b></td>
              <td class="num">{r.countries ?? '—'}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.weeks ?? '—'}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.atOnce ?? '—'}</td>
              <td class="fine">{r.firstWeek ?? '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section>
    <h2>What this is, and what it is not</h2>
    <p class="fine">
      <b>&ldquo;At once&rdquo; is not the same as &ldquo;countries&rdquo;.</b> Countries counts every
      market that ever listed the film. At once is the most it held in any single week. A film that
      charted in 40 countries one after another and one that charted in 40 on the same Tuesday
      are different events, and the two columns keep them apart.
    </p>
    <p class="fine"><b>Not separated here.</b> {data.notSeparated}</p>
    <p class="fine">{data.cannotAnswer}</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to walk from here</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/netflix-top-10-korean-drama">Korean series on Netflix, the same counts</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/most-popular">Three measures of &ldquo;most popular&rdquo; that disagree</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/underrated">The ones that ran and ran, almost nowhere</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/where-to-watch">Where each title could actually be watched</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/firms">The companies behind them</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/actors">The people in them</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/titles">Every Korean title we hold</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwMethodLimits
    method={`Netflix's own weekly top 10 files by country, ${data.weekFrom} to ${data.weekTo}, across ${data.marketCount} countries. Netflix publishes two separate weekly lists, one for films and one for series; this page uses the films list only. A title that appears on both lists is two different works sharing a name, and we do not add them together — where we could not establish which work is the Korean one, we publish neither set of numbers.`}
    limitation={`A chart place is not a viewing figure. Netflix publishes no hours or viewers by country, so nothing here says how many people watched, and a small market counts the same as a large one. Netflix states no country of production, so which titles count as Korean is our inference from the title text and from Wikidata. Our rows also do not say whether a film opened in cinemas before reaching Netflix, so theatrical releases and Netflix originals sit in the same table.`}
  />

  <style>
    .lead { font-size: 1.08rem; }
    .warn { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: .7rem 1rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 2rem;
      font-size: .95rem; }
    h2 { font-size: 1.12rem; margin: 2.4rem 0 .3rem; }
    .sub { color: var(--muted); font-size: .92rem; margin: 0 0 .8rem; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .92rem; }
    table.cmp, table.yr { max-width: 34rem; }
    th, td { text-align: left; padding: .4rem .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
    th.num, td.num { text-align: right; }
    .nm { font-weight: 600; }
    .fine { color: var(--muted); font-size: .86rem; }
    .bar { display: inline-block; height: .5rem; width: var(--w); max-width: 9rem;
      background: var(--accent); opacity: .35; margin-left: .5rem; vertical-align: middle; }
  </style>
</WikiTip>
